# Can the king catch the pawn?
teaches: ["endgames/square-rule","endgames/opposition"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What is the rule of the square? What is the opposition, and what are key squares?"
next: {"title":"What must you know about rook endings?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/24-what-must-you-know-about-rook-endings.cast"}

## Warm-up: the king as a target
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2q1r1k/pb2Nppp/3p4/7Q/8/3R4/PP3PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qxh7+, Kxh7, Rh3#]}
      - {fen: r4rk1/pb1q1ppp/2n5/8/8/2NB4/PPP2PPP/2QR1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kxh7, Rxd7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: can a king catch a running pawn? Kings and pawns, where one move decides the game. First, two warm-ups.
    draw position

B: Black's king has three pawns in front of him. Looks safe to me.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_g7 piece_h7

A: Look at the knight on e7. It already guards g8 and g6.
    highlight piece_e7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Checkmate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Give up the queen, with check.","Then the rook on d3 swings to the h-file."] themes ["king attack","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["strategy/king-attack","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen takes h7, check: the king must take, and rook to h3 is mate. The knight covers g8 and g6.
        wrong: Which check strips away the king's pawn cover?

B: A whole queen, just to open the h-file.
    highlight piece_h3

A: A king attack counts the king's squares, not the material.
    point at.ref piece_h7 gesture circle

A: Second one. Black's queen on d7 looks safe enough.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Bishop takes h7 is check, but the king just takes it.
    highlight piece_d3

A: Then look at what the bishop left behind.
    highlight piece_d1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A check that uncovers an attack.","What stands behind the bishop on d3?"] themes ["discovered attack","combination"] voice a teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack","calculation/combination"]
        right: Bishop takes h7, check: the king takes it, and rook takes d7. A bishop for the queen.
        wrong: Look for a bishop move that gives check.

B: The check gave Black no time to save the queen.
    highlight piece_d7

A: In both, the king was the target. Today the queens are gone, and the king stops hiding: it fights.
    highlight piece_h7

## The square and the opposition
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/8/8/8/P4k2/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Ke5, a5], highlights: [a4, a5, a6, a7, a8, b4, b5, b6, b7, b8, c4, c5, c6, c7, c8, d4, d5, d6, d7, d8, e4, e5, e6, e7, e8]}
      - {fen: 8/8/8/P3k3/8/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 2, moves: [Kd6, a6], highlights: [a5, a6, a7, a8, b5, b6, b7, b8, c5, c6, c7, c8, d5, d6, d7, d8]}
      - {fen: 8/8/P2k4/8/8/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 3, moves: [Kc7, a7], highlights: [a6, a7, a8, b6, b7, b8, c6, c7, c8]}
      - {fen: 8/P1k5/8/8/8/8/6K1/8 b - - 0 4, moves: [Kb7], highlights: [a7, a8, b7, b8]}
      - {fen: 8/8/8/8/P4k2/8/6K1/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [a5, Ke5, a6, Kd6, a7, Kc7, a8=Q], highlights: [a5, a6, a7, a8, b5, b6, b7, b8, c5, c6, c7, c8, d5, d6, d7, d8]}
      - {fen: 8/3k4/8/3K4/3P4/8/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Kc5, Kc7], highlights: [c6, d6, e6]}
      - {fen: 8/3k4/8/3K4/3P4/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Kc7, Ke6], highlights: [c6, d6, e6]}
      - {fen: 8/8/4k3/8/8/4PK2/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ke4, Kd6, Kf5, Ke7, Ke5, Kd7, Kf6, Kc6, e4, Kd7, e5], highlights: [d5, e5, f5], arrows: [{from: e3, to: e4}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: square
      title: Rule of the square
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: A king can catch a lone passed pawn if it is inside, or can move into, the square whose side runs from the pawn to its promotion rank; a pawn on its starting rank is counted from one square further on.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_the_square
    - id: opposition
      title: Opposition (chess)
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Two kings on the same file or rank with one square between are in direct opposition; the side not to move has it, and the other king must give way.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opposition_(chess)
    - id: keysq
      title: Key square
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 'For a non-rook pawn on its own half of the board, the three squares two ranks in front of it are key squares: the attacking king on one of them wins, whoever is to move.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_square
    - id: tb
      title: Lichess Syzygy tablebase
      authors: Lichess
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Every king-and-pawn position in this part checked: the results of each move as stated.'
      url: https://tablebase.lichess.ovh

    card title "The square"

A: Kings and one pawn. White's pawn runs for a8, and it's Black to move.
    draw position

B: The pawn needs four moves, the king needs five. The pawn wins.
    highlight piece_a4

A: Count again: Black moves first, and his king only has to reach b7. Four moves, not five.
    point at.ref sq_b7 gesture circle

A: Easier still: draw the paw (@draw hl_a4 hl_a5 hl_a6 hl_a7 hl_a8 hl_b4 hl_b5 hl_b6 hl_b7 hl_b8 hl_c4 hl_c5 hl_c6 hl_c7 hl_c8 hl_d4 hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_d7 hl_d8 hl_e4 hl_e5 hl_e6 hl_e7 hl_e8 parallel true@)n's square, from the pawn to the last rank, and as wide again.

A: If the king can step inside it, he catches the pawn.
    highlight piece_f4

B: King to e5. He's in.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Each pawn step shrinks the square, and the king stays inside. The pawn falls.
    blocking false

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 0.8

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.3

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 0.8

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 0.8

    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.3

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 0.8

    animate plies_shown 2 duration 0.8

    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.3

    animate plies_shown 1 duration 0.8

    pause 0.5

    erase hl_a4 hl_a5 hl_a6 hl_a7 hl_a8 hl_b4 hl_b5 hl_b6 hl_b7 hl_b8 hl_c4 hl_c5 hl_c6 hl_c7 hl_c8 hl_d4 hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_d7 hl_d8 hl_e4 hl_e5 hl_e6 hl_e7 hl_e8

A: Same position, but now it's White to move. Can Black's king still catch it?
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win." widget chess line 1 hints ["Who moves first matters.","Push the pawn before the king steps in."] themes ["square rule"] voice a
        right: Pawn to a5: the pawn moves first, so the king is one step too late.
        wrong: Try the pawn's square after a pawn move.

A: Its square now runs from a5 to d8, and the king is left outside.
    draw hl_a5 hl_a6 hl_a7 hl_a8 hl_b5 hl_b6 hl_b7 hl_b8 hl_c5 hl_c6 hl_c7 hl_c8 hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_d7 hl_d8 parallel true

B: One move earlier, and the whole game changes.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 4

A: One warning: the square is for a pawn alone. If its own king can help, count it out.
    highlight piece_g2

    erase hl_a5 hl_a6 hl_a7 hl_a8 hl_b5 hl_b6 hl_b7 hl_b8 hl_c5 hl_c6 hl_c7 hl_c8 hl_d5 hl_d6 hl_d7 hl_d8

    erase card_1_title

    card title "The opposition"

A: Now the kings are close, and White's king escorts the pawn.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Get the king onto a key square, two ranks in front of the pawn, and the pawn queens, whoever is to move.
    draw hl_c6 hl_d6 hl_e6 parallel true

B: Easy. King to c5, and round he goes.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Black just follows. Kings face to face, one square between: that's the opposition.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Whoever must move has to give way. Here it's White, so it's a draw.
    highlight piece_c5 piece_c7

A: Give Black the move instead.
    animate position_shown 6 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Now his king must step aside,
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

B: and mine walks onto e6. A key square.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

    erase hl_c6 hl_d6 hl_e6

A: Your turn. Pawn on e3:
    animate position_shown 7 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: its key squares are d5, e5 and f5.
    draw hl_d5 hl_e5 hl_f5 parallel true

B: I'd push it to e4, guarded by the king.
    draw arrow_0

    erase arrow_0

A: Then the key squares move up with it, out of your king's reach. Only a draw.
    highlight piece_e6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win." widget chess line 1 hints ["Take the opposition.","Put your king in front of the pawn."] themes ["opposition","key squares"] voice a
        right: King to e4: face to face, so Black must give way, and your king steps onto a key square.
        wrong: Pushing first only draws. Where does your king face Black's?

A: Opposition first, then a key square,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.6

A: and the pawn walks in.
    animate plies_shown 11 duration 5

## Your turn: catch it or escort it
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Five for five. Your king is ready for the ending.
    good: Most of them. Draw the square, then count again.
    poor: Pawn endings are exact. The misses come back until they stick.
    none: The kings will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 8/8/8/8/2P5/7k/8/7K b - - 0 1, moves: [Kg4, c5, Kf5, c6, Ke6, c7, Kd7, c8=Q+, Kxc8], flip: true}
      - {fen: 8/5k2/8/3K4/4P3/8/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Kd6]}
      - {fen: 2k5/8/8/2K5/2P5/8/8/8 b - - 0 1, moves: [Kc7], flip: true}
      - {fen: 8/8/5p2/2k4r/8/6P1/5PK1/R7 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ra5+, Kd4, Rxh5]}
      - {fen: 7K/8/k1P5/7p/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1, moves: [Kg7, h4, Kf6, Kb6, Ke5, Kxc6, Kf4, h3, Kg3, h2, Kxh2]}
      - {fen: 8/8/8/4k3/8/3K4/4P3/8 w - - 0 1}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: reti
      title: Réti endgame study
      authors: Richard Réti
      year: 1921
      finding: 'White to play and draw: 1.Kg7 h4 2.Kf6 Kb6 3.Ke5 — the king heads for both pawns at once along the diagonal.'
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R%C3%A9ti_endgame_study
    - id: tb
      title: Lichess Syzygy tablebase
      authors: Lichess
      year: 2026
      finding: Every king-and-pawn puzzle here has exactly one saving or winning first move; the goal position is a win with best play.
      url: https://tablebase.lichess.ovh

    card title "Your turn"

A: Your turn: five endings, and a score at the end. First, the board has turned: you're Black.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Save the game." widget chess line 1 hints ["Draw the pawn's square.","Step inside it."] themes ["square rule"] voice a test t23
        right: King to g4: the only step into the square. Then down the diagonal, and the pawn falls.
        wrong: Which king move lands inside the pawn's square?

    animate plies_shown 9 duration 2.5

A: Back to White. Pawn on e4.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win." widget chess line true hints ["Where are the pawn's key squares?","Two ranks in front of the pawn."] themes ["key squares"] voice a test t23
        right: King to d6: a key square, and the pawn will queen. Pushing to e5 first only draws.
        wrong: Think king first, not pawn.

A: The board turns again. Now you defend.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Hold the draw." widget chess line true hints ["Take the opposition.","Face White's king."] themes ["opposition","defence"] voice a test t23
        right: King to c7: face to face, and White must give way. Anywhere else, his king reaches b6, c6 or d6.
        wrong: Which square keeps White's king off the key squares?

A: Now a review from an earlier day. White to move.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Line up king and rook.","A check along the fifth rank."] themes ["skewer"] voice a test t23 teaches ["tactics/skewer"]
        right: Rook to a5, check: the king steps off the rank, and the rook on h5 falls.
        wrong: Is there a check that hits something behind the king?

A: Last, a famous study by Richard Réti, from 1921.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: My king is miles from that pawn. Hopeless.
    highlight piece_h8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Draw." widget chess line 5 hints ["Your king can chase two goals at once.","Walk down the diagonal."] themes ["square rule"] voice a test t23
        right: King to g7, then f6: heading for both pawns at once.
        wrong: Look for a king move that helps your own pawn too.

A: Black's king takes your pawn, and yours reaches his just in time.
    animate plies_shown 11 duration 5

    hide position

A: You scored {t23.score} of {t23.total}.
    results test t23

    show position

A: Now play one out. Your king on d3, the pawn on e2, White to move.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: Make a queen within twenty moves, against the computer. Take the opposition first.
    explore activity vs_computer goal.kind promote goal.within 20 store kp

    hide position

A: In the ending the king stops hiding and fights:
    text w_square "square" x 500 y 470 font_size 84
    text w_opp "opposition" x 500 y 340 font_size 84
    parallel true

A: the square says if he's in time, and the opposition says who gets through.
    highlight w_square w_opp

    erase w_square w_opp

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 400 font_size 130

